          players: 'Three players; no partnerships. The game is played mainly in Armenia (e.g. Gyumri). Deal, bidding and play are clockwise; each player plays for themselves toward 121 points.',
          deck: '32 cards (remove 2s through 6s).',
          equipment: '',
          dealing: "Each player receives 10 cards (often dealt 2 at a time). 2 cards are placed face-down in the 'Prikup'.",
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        rules: {
          objective: 'Be the first player to reach 121 cumulative points.',
          gameplay: 'Deal 10 cards to each player and 2 face-down to the prikup. Players bid by passing or naming a trump suit (Paston, Glitch, Dineri, Gupa, Paston grkha). Highest bidder becomes declarer, takes the prikup, discards two cards, and names trump. Declarer must take at least six tricks. Opponents may say whist (defend) or pass. Play: declarer leads; follow suit if possible, otherwise play a trump. Highest trump or highest card of led suit wins the trick; winner leads next. Scores are cumulative; game ends at 121 points.',
          keyRules: [
            'Follow suit if possible; if void, must play a trump.',
            'Declarer must take at least six tricks or scores penalty.',
            'Trump ranking: Paston (spades) lowest, then Glitch, Dineri, Gupa, Paston grkha (best spades) highest.',
            'When all pass in bidding, holder of Jack of spades (or 7 of spades if Jack in prikup) becomes declarer.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: "Prioritize the Prikup! If you have a solid hand with 4 trumps, bid to get the extra 2 cards. Those 2 cards can fix a weak suit or give you the Ace you need to win your 6th trick. Don't be too aggressive without the Jack of your suit; the Jack is a powerful tool for 'Drawing' opponent trumps early.",
          intermediate: "The 'Paston Grkha' (No-Trump) Risk: Only bid Grkha if you have Aces and Kings across at least three suits. Since there is no trump to save you, one long suit held by an opponent can destroy your contract. Conversely, if you are defending against a Grkha bid, try to figure out which suit the declarer discarded and lead precisely that suit.",
          advanced: "Defensive Coordination: Like in Preferans, the two defenders should play 'Against the Declarer'. If your partner leads a suit, and you have the King while the Declarer has the Ace, play low to force the Ace out. Your goal is to make the Declarer use their power cards for small tricks, leaving them vulnerable to your 'Long Suit' in the final 4 tricks.",
          tips: [
            "The 7 of Spades is a 'Redeal' card in many Armenian circlesâ€”check your local house rules!",
            'If you win only 5 tricks, you lose the 10 points. 6 is the magic number.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
